FINAL ANSWER:
**Two people own most of the code still in the repo: Troy Goode and Douglas Christopher Wilson. Between them they hold about 72% of the surviving lines.** These figures come from `surviving-lines`, which runs `git blame -w -M -C` at HEAD (9f866e1, dated 2026-04-12). The repo has 1,664 non-blank lines, or 1,987 counting blank lines.

| Author | Non-blank lines (share) | Counting blank lines (share) | Commits (share) | Last commit |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Troy Goode (2 addresses, merged) | 649 (39.0%) | 810 (40.8%) | 95 (30.1%) | 2026-04-10 |
| Douglas Christopher Wilson | 551 (33.1%) | 629 (31.7%) | 143 (45.3%) | 2021-09-07 |
| Íñigo Marquínez Prado | 80 (4.8%) | 95 (4.8%) | 2 (0.6%) | 2024-05-14 |
| Jon Church | 59 (3.5%) | 72 (3.6%) | 3 (0.9%) | 2026-01-16 |
| Sebastian Beltran | 57 (3.4%) | 67 (3.4%) | 2 (0.6%) | 2025-07-23 |
| Ulises Gascón | 46 (2.8%) | 52 (2.6%) | 3 (0.9%) | 2026-01-22 |
| Ignacio Mangas | 38 (2.3%) | 44 (2.2%) | 2 (0.6%) | 2025-12-01 |
| Alejandro Caravaca Puchades | 30 (1.8%) | 34 (1.7%) | 5 (1.6%) | 2015-05-28 |
| Linus Unnebäck | 26 (1.6%) | 26 (1.3%) | 1 (0.3%) | 2016-11-07 |
| Ollie Buck | 14 (0.8%) | 17 (0.9%) | 1 (0.3%) | 2019-06-05 |

The other 52 authors hold 6.9% together. The largest of them is Eric di Domenico, with 0.7%.

The tool's summary line: "Troy Goode (2 addresses) holds 649 non-blank lines (39.0%), 810 counting blank lines (40.8%)".

**Lines versus commits:** Douglas Christopher Wilson made the most commits (45.3%) but holds fewer surviving lines (33.1%) than Troy Goode. Wilson's last commit was in September 2021.

## What affects these numbers
- **Reformat commit left out:** `62628c0` ("style: reformat js with 4-space indent and double quotes", by Fiona Formatter) rewrote 1,016 lines in 4 files. It is listed in `.git-blame-ignore-revs`, and the tool reads that file, so the lines stay with the people who wrote them. Without that file, the formatter would look like a major owner.
- **Troy Goode is merged (a guess):** the merge assumes <email redacted> and troy@laptop.example are the same person, based only on name and address. Unmerged, the two addresses hold:
  - <email redacted>: 605 non-blank lines (36.4%), 762 counting blank lines (38.3%).
  - troy@laptop.example: 44 lines.

  Nothing was written to `.mailmap`.
- **Alejandro Caravaca Puchades:** the tool already counts "alex94puchades" (same address) as this person.
- **Possible mechanical rewrites still counted:** 7 more commits look mechanical but were not set aside. Examples are `739dc11` ("docs: reformat license", Wilson, 17 lines) and `fcd2b56` (ESLint cleanup, Goode, 14 lines). They are small and don't change the ranking.
- **Bot left out:** dependabot[bot] (10 lines, 11 commits) gets no credit.
- **Commits made by GitHub:** 19 commits were committed by GitHub on someone else's behalf, for example `de19afc` (Íñigo Marquínez Prado's CI scorecard work). Those lines are credited to the person who wrote them.
- **Import at the start:** history begins on 2013-01-30 with a commit that adds 42% of the files. Lines older than that go to whoever imported them.

## Concentration and risk
- **Bus factor: 2.** This is the smallest number of authors holding 50% of the lines, and it is 2 whether or not blank lines are counted. Goode and Wilson together hold 72.1% of non-blank lines (72.4% counting blank lines). Reaching 80% takes 4 authors with the Goode merge, or 5 without it.
- **Active bus factor: 1.** This one is counted only on the lines of people who committed in the last 365 days. Troy Goode alone holds half of those lines, which makes him a single point of failure.
- **Activity:** as of 2026-04-12, 10 of 45 authors committed in the last 365 days, and they hold 51.8% of the lines. The other 48.2% belong to people with no commit in that time, mostly Wilson.
- **Orphaned files:** a separate risk. In 6 of the 19 files (7.8% of the lines), the top owner is inactive and no active person holds 20%. Examples are `.github/workflows/scorecard.yml` (Íñigo Marquínez Prado), `HISTORY.md` and `LICENSE` (both Wilson). This flags files to check, not proof that nobody knows them.
- **Top owner of each directory:**
  - `lib/`: Troy Goode, 61.7%
  - `test/`: Troy Goode, 47.3%
  - `.github/`: Douglas Christopher Wilson, 30.2%
  - root files: Douglas Christopher Wilson, 29.2%

**Who to ask:** Troy Goode is the top owner and is still active, so he is both the main source of knowledge and the practical contact. Wilson holds a third of the code but has no commit since 2021.

These counts show whose lines survive. They say nothing about review work, design, or quality.

=== ANSWER C ===
COMMANDS RUN:
 - Bash: cat .git-blame-ignore-revs; git log --format='%h %an <%ae> %ad %s' --date=short | head -20; git log --oneline | wc -l; git ls-files | head -50; for c in 62628c0 b2005d9 02afce3; do git show --stat --format='%h %an %s' $c | tail -15; done
 - Bash: git show b2005d9 | head -60; git show 62628c0 -w --stat | tail -3; ls -a; git log --format='%an <%ae>' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -30
 - Bash: run(){ for f in $(git ls-files); do git blame --line-porcelain -w "$@" HEAD -- "$f" | sed -n 's/^author //p'; done | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -12; echo ---; }; echo RAW; run; echo IGNORE-REFORMAT; run --ignore-revs-file .git-blame-ignore-revs; echo IGNORE-BOTH; run --ignore-revs-file .git-blame-ignore-revs --ignore-rev b2005d9; echo CODE-ONLY lib+test; for f in lib/index.js test/*.js test/support/env.js; do git blame --line-porcelain -w --ignore-revs-file .git-blame-ignore-revs --ignore-rev b2005d9 HEAD -- "$f" | sed -n 's/^author //p'; done | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head; echo; for f in lib/index.js; do git blame --line-porcelain -w --ignore-revs-file .git-blame-ignore-revs --ig
 - Bash: for f in $(git ls-files); do git blame --line-porcelain -w --ignore-revs-file .git-blame-ignore-revs HEAD -- "$f" | sed -n 's/^author-mail //p'; done | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -5; git ls-files | xargs cat | wc -l; for f in $(git ls-files); do echo "$f $(git blame --line-porcelain -w --ignore-revs-file .git-blame-ignore-revs HEAD -- "$f" | sed -n 's/^author //p' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -1)"; done
FINAL ANSWER:
Troy Goode owns most of the code still in the repo, and Douglas Christopher Wilson is close behind. I ran `git blame` on every tracked file at HEAD (1,995 lines) and counted who wrote each surviving line. Getting a fair count took two corrections:

1. **Skipping the reformat commit.** Commit `62628c0` from "Fiona Formatter" re-indented and re-quoted the JS files. A plain blame credits Fiona with 293 lines she didn't write. The repo's `.git-blame-ignore-revs` lists that commit, so I skipped it (along with whitespace-only changes), which gives those lines back to their original authors.
2. **Combining Troy's two email addresses.** Troy commits as both `<email redacted>` and `troy@laptop.example`, so I counted them together. The laptop address accounts for 48 lines: all of the new `test/vary.js` (36 lines) and 12 `// checked` comments added to the ends of existing lines in `lib/index.js`. Skipping that comment commit too barely changes the totals.

**Lines still present, by author:**

| Author | Lines | Share |
|---|---|---|
| Troy Goode | ~746 | ~37% |
| Douglas Christopher Wilson | 645 | ~32% |
| Íñigo Marquínez Prado | 95 | ~5% |
| Jon Church | 72 | ~4% |
| Sebastian Beltran | 67 | ~3% |
| Ignacio Mangas | 54 | ~3% |
| Ulises Gascón | 52 | ~3% |
| Everyone else | under 30 each | — |

**Which files each person owns:**
- **Troy:** the actual library and most of the tests. He wrote 157 of the roughly 240 lines in `lib/index.js` (about two-thirds; Douglas is next with 22). He also has the most surviving lines in `README.md`, `test/error-response.js`, `test/example-app.js`, `test/issue-2.js` and `test/vary.js`.
- **Douglas:** the project infrastructure. That's `test/test.js` (365 lines), the CI workflow, `package.json`, `HISTORY.md`, `LICENSE` and the lint config.
- **Smaller owners:** Íñigo (the scorecard workflow), Sebastian (CodeQL), Ulises (dependabot config), and Jon and Ignacio (docs and CI changes).

By commit count Douglas leads, 143 to Troy's 122. By surviving lines Troy is ahead, mainly because the library code is his. Fiona, the top author in a plain blame of the JS files, owns essentially nothing apart from the two-line ignore-revs file.
